tor>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-16-rerum/</guid><description>A Python library for symbolic computation with a readable DSL, pattern matching, and a security model that separates rules from computation.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-16-rerum/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Programming Languages</media:title></media:content><category>symbolic-computation</category><category>term-rewriting</category><category>pattern-matching</category><category>dsl</category><category>python</category><category>computer-algebra</category><category>functional-programming</category><category>sicp</category></item><item><title>chop: When Every Command Returns the Same Kind of Thing</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-07-15-chop/</link><pubDate>Tue, 15 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-07-15-chop/</guid><description>27 image commands, one constraint: read JSON, write JSON. The closure property as a generative design principle.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-07-15-chop/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Programming Languages</media:title></media:content><category>sicp</category><category>dsl</category><category>unix-philosophy</category><category>python</category><category>image-processing</category><category>closure-property</category><category>lazy-evaluation</category></item><item><title>JSL: A Functional Language Where Code Is JSON</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-11-jsl/</link><pubDate>Wed, 20 Nov 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-11-jsl/</guid><description>A Lisp-like functional language designed for network transmission. JSL makes JSON serialization a first-class design principle, so closures, continuations, and entire computation states can travel over the wire.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-11-jsl/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Programming Languages</media:title></media:content><category>Python</category><category>programming-language</category><category>functional-programming</category><category>distributed-computing</category><category>Lisp</category><category>JSON</category><category>homoiconicity</category><category>serialization</category><category>bytecode</category></item><item><title>Apertures: A Language with Holes</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-04-01-aperture/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Apr 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-04-01-aperture/</guid><description>When the problem is coordinating computation across parties who can&amp;#39;t share data, the SICP move is to build a language for it. Apertures adds one primitive — holes — to a Lisp, and gets pausable, resumable evaluation for free.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-04-01-aperture/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Programming Languages</media:title></media:content><category>sicp</category><category>dsl</category><category>metalinguistic-abstraction</category><category>partial-evaluation</category><category>coordination</category><category>programming-languages</category></item></channel></rss>